// --- A value chip's internal hierarchy -----------------------------------
//
// One block, and only the four declarations that decide which half of a chip
// a reader sees first. Everything else about either half — background,
// padding, radius, font — is left where it already lives, because those
// declarations are duplicated across `flow.styl` and two blocks of
// `text_editor.styl` and re-stating them here would silently change the
// debugger's own chips.
//
// The hierarchy was inverted. The name carried
// `colors-ui-text-primary-body` (#f3f3f3) and the value inherited the editor's
// body colour, so the LABEL was the brightest thing in the chip and the number
// it labels was the dimmest. Three reviewers said so from two directions: "the
// value half is smaller and dimmer than the name half — hierarchy inverted";
// "same fill, same weight, distinguished only by a wide space, so
// `<contribution> 0` reads as one token"; and, measured off a capture, "name
// #E2E2E2, value #B5BDC5 … the *name* reads as the more important half". A
// reviewer of the result, shown nothing about what had changed, answered the
// same question with "the value reads as more important: brighter, bold, in
// its own inset box. The split is clearly visible as a tone step between
// halves."
//
// Both hosts get it, because both had the inversion, and because
// DeepReview-GUI.md §5.3 forbids a review-specific inline style. The
// `.code-editor` variants are listed because `text_editor.styl` carries a copy
// of the name rule at that scope which a bare selector loses to;
// `codetracer.styl` imports `text_editor` before `flow`, so at equal
// specificity these are the declarations that land.
.flow-parallel-value-name,
.code-editor .flow-parallel-value-name
  color: colors-ui-text-primary-body-subtle
  // The split between the two halves. Without it the pair reads as one token.
  // A hairline is enough: a full border would make each chip read as two.
  border-right: 1px solid colors-ui-border-secondary

.flow-parallel-value-box,
.code-editor .flow-parallel-value-box
  color: colors-ui-text-primary-body
  font-weight: 600
  // CLIPPING — the chip must never draw outside its own box.
  //
  // These four declarations were live until a69766dd ("New design system
  // implementation") commented out the whole `.flow-parallel-value-box`
  // block below, taking `white-space`/`overflow`/`text-overflow` with it.
  // The inline `max-width: 30ch` that `ui/flow.nim` sets on long values
  // survived, so the box got NARROW while the text stayed FULL LENGTH:
  // with the default `white-space: normal` the value wrapped onto extra
  // lines instead of being cut off, and since a chip is drawn in a Monaco
  // view zone laid over the source, those extra lines overdrew the code
  // beneath — "the value overflows visibly into the next line ... a major
  // overdraw and everything becomes unreadable".
  //
  // No `min-width: 0` here, though the chip IS a flex item of
  // `.ct-omni-value` (`display: flex`) and a flex item's automatic minimum
  // size normally pins it to its min-content width — which, under `nowrap`,
  // would be the whole string. It was added on that reasoning and then
  // measured: identical box (210px wide, 26px tall) with and without it.
  // The automatic minimum size is clamped by a specified `max-width`, and
  // `ui/flow.nim` sets `max-width: 30ch` inline on exactly the long values
  // this rule has to clip. So the declaration was inert, and inert
  // declarations invite the next reader to preserve them.
  //
  // Only the clipping declarations are restored. The rest of the commented
  // block (padding, radius, font, background) is deliberately NOT revived —
  // `.ct-omni-value` supplies the chip's appearance now, and re-stating it
  // here would change how every chip looks.
  white-space: nowrap
  overflow: hidden
  text-overflow: ellipsis

